# Native engines only. Appending to a list inside a helper function, called
# once per iteration of a tail-recursive loop, copies the whole list on every
# call, so n pushes cost O(n^2) time.
#
# Should: run in a few milliseconds, as it does on the interpreter and as the
# same loop does when `push` is written inline in `fill` instead of in `bump`.
#
# Does (kanso play, or kanso build at either tier):
#   n = 10000   ~2 s       KANSO_COUNTERS=1 at n = 3000 shows
#   n = 40000   > 20 s     evac_bytes=144143632 push_mut_slow=3000
# --interp: 0.01 s at n = 40000.
#
# The counters say each call to `bump` is a beat whose result is evacuated,
# and the evacuated result is the whole list.
fn bump xs i
  push xs i

fn fill xs 0
  xs

fn fill xs n
  fill (bump xs n) (n - 1)

print "{length (fill [] 10000)}"
